				<article class="content">
					<!-- WHAT IS A BIOME -->
					<section id="what-is-a-biome" class="major-section">
						<h2 class="section-title">🌿 Building and Using Biomes</h2>
						<p>In Untamed, a Biome isn't just a hunting ground — it's a living system.</p>
						<p>It breathes, grows, suffers, and changes alongside the creatures and hunters inside it.</p>
						<p>Whether you use the pre-built biomes (like the Forest Cluster) or create your own, every biome should feel like a real ecosystem — shaped by its monsters, weather, resources, and conflicts.</p>

						<h3>🌎 What is a Biome?</h3>
						<ul class="check-list">
							<li>A collection of connected sectors (zones or regions).</li>
							<li>A network of resources, creatures, and environmental hazards.</li>
							<li>A dynamic stage where random events and monster migrations happen.</li>
							<li>A home for the major monsters (and a few surprises).</li>
						</ul>
						<p>Think of a biome like a living boardgame — players move across sectors, weather changes the rules, and monsters rewrite the board as they migrate or clash.</p>
					</section>

					<!-- CORE FEATURES -->
					<section id="core-features" class="major-section">
						<h2 class="section-title">🗺️ Core Biome Features to Track</h2>
						<p>Every biome you create or run should ideally have:</p>
						<div class="table-wrapper">
							<table class="craft-table">
								<thead>
									<tr>
										<th>Feature</th>
										<th>Purpose</th>
									</tr>
								</thead>
								<tbody>
									<tr>
										<td>Sector Map</td>
										<td>Break the biome into named zones (ex: Canopy Heights, River Basin, etc.). Each zone has its own terrain, hazards, and monster behaviors.</td>
									</tr>
									<tr>
										<td>Resources</td>
										<td>Define gathering spots: herbs, crafting minerals, hazards like mud pits or cliff falls.</td>
									</tr>
									<tr>
										<td>Small Creatures</td>
										<td>Add flavor, low-risk resource farming, and hints about major predators. (Ex: Boars, Raptors, Mudcrawlers.)</td>
									</tr>
									<tr>
										<td>Major Monsters</td>
										<td>Pick a roster of big monsters that live here. Include at least one Apex Monster for endgame hunts.</td>
									</tr>
									<tr>
										<td>Random Event Tables</td>
										<td>Create or use a 1d100 table for weather changes, sudden dangers, or monster behaviors.</td>
									</tr>
									<tr>
										<td>Ecology Shifts</td>
										<td>Prepare a 1d6 table for larger world changes — floods, migrations, blooms, disasters — that permanently alter the map over time.</td>
									</tr>
								</tbody>
							</table>
						</div>
					</section>

					<!-- RUNNING A BIOME HUNT -->
					<section id="running-a-biome-hunt" class="major-section">
						<h2 class="section-title">🎯 Running a Biome Hunt</h2>
						<p>When a session takes place inside a biome, you'll be balancing exploration, combat, and environmental changes.</p>

						<h3>Key GM Moves:</h3>
						<ul>
							<li><strong>Use Random Events:</strong> Roll or select a random event every 1–2 hours of in-game exploration to keep the environment evolving.</li>
							<li><strong>Trigger Monster Migrations:</strong> When major monsters are killed, severely wounded, or territories are disrupted, move surviving creatures into new sectors.</li>
							<li><strong>Shift the Biome Over Time:</strong> After 2–3 major hunts (or a major Apex battle), roll an Ecology Shift. This shows how the biome responds to player actions — flooding, droughts, new predators rising, or entire regions becoming unstable.</li>
						</ul>
					</section>

					<!-- DESIGN TIPS -->
					<section id="design-tips" class="major-section">
						<h2 class="section-title">🧠 GM Tips for Biome Design</h2>

						<ul class="check-list">
							<li><strong>Theme First:</strong><br />Start by picking a real-world inspiration (Mediterranean Coast, Volcanic Highlands, Coral Reef) and build your sectors and monsters to match.</li>
							<li><strong>Big Monsters Shape the Map:</strong><br />Where the monsters live changes where it's safe — and where it's not.</li>
							<li><strong>More Than Fights:</strong><br />Give players reasons to explore beyond just hunting: rare materials, hidden ruins, NPC camps, monster signs.</li>
							<li><strong>Dynamic World = Immersion:</strong><br />Don't be afraid to let monsters migrate, territories collapse, or weather destroy paths — it makes victories feel earned.</li>
						</ul>
					</section>

					<!-- HOMEBREW CHECKLIST -->
					<section id="homebrew-checklist" class="major-section">
						<h2 class="section-title">🔥 Quick Checklist for Homebrew Biomes</h2>
						<div class="table-wrapper">
							<table class="craft-table">
								<thead>
									<tr>
										<th>Step</th>
										<th>Goal</th>
									</tr>
								</thead>
								<tbody>
									<tr>
										<td>1. Name and theme your biome</td>
										<td>"Ashen Peaks" (volcano zone) / "Sapphire Depths" (coral reef)</td>
									</tr>
									<tr>
										<td>2. Draw 4–8 Sectors</td>
										<td>Each should have terrain, dangers, and some resources.</td>
									</tr>
									<tr>
										<td>3. Pick 4–6 Major Monsters</td>
										<td>At least one Apex-level creature included.</td>
									</tr>
									<tr>
										<td>4. Create Small Creatures &amp; Resources</td>
										<td>Flavor, gathering, world-building.</td>
									</tr>
									<tr>
										<td>5. Build Random Event Table</td>
										<td>1d100 or 1d20 depending on how detailed you want it.</td>
									</tr>
									<tr>
										<td>6. Build Ecology Shift Table</td>
										<td>1d6 to trigger major changes over time.</td>
									</tr>
									<tr>
										<td>7. Connect it all with "natural logic"</td>
										<td>Why do creatures live here? How do they interact? What happens if the Apex falls?</td>
								</tr>
								</tbody>
							</table>
						</div>
					</section>

					<!-- VILLAGES -->
					<section id="villages" class="major-section">
						<h2 class="section-title">🏘️ Village Structure &amp; Ecosystem Support</h2>
						<p>In Untamed, hunter villages form the lifeline between civilization and the wilds. Whether perched on cliffside crags or nestled in river deltas, they all follow a similar foundational layout to support ongoing survival, trade, and hunting.</p>

						<p>These settlements act as:</p>
						<ul>
							<li>Safe zones for rest, recovery, and crafting.</li>
							<li>Information hubs to learn about monster behaviors and regional changes.</li>
							<li>Quest and supply stations that evolve based on player actions and the shifting world.</li>
						</ul>

						<h3>🌱 Common Infrastructure (All Villages Share These Roles)</h3>
						<div class="table-wrapper">
							<table class="craft-table">
								<thead>
									<tr>
										<th>Role</th>
										<th>Function</th>
										<th>Hooks &amp; Notes</th>
									</tr>
								</thead>
								<tbody>
									<tr>
										<td>Forge Master</td>
										<td>Crafts and upgrades gear from monster parts and rare materials.</td>
										<td>Unlocks new gear with each major monster defeated. May offer personal challenges or forge-specific tasks.</td>
									</tr>
									<tr>
										<td>Supplier / Provisioner</td>
										<td>Provides basic supplies: traps, potions, food, and ammo.</td>
										<td>Inventory expands with trade routes or if players aid caravans or traders.</td>
									</tr>
									<tr>
										<td>Alchemist / Herbalist</td>
										<td>Crafts potions, buffs, cures. May offer unique concoctions based on regional flora/fauna.</td>
										<td>May have personal goals or need rare ingredients to upgrade potion tiers.</td>
									</tr>
									<tr>
										<td>Quest Giver / Elder / Hunt Warden</td>
										<td>Gives out bounties, story quests, and tracks biome health &amp; Apex behavior.</td>
										<td>Crucial for advancing regional narratives and unlocking major encounters.</td>
									</tr>
									<tr>
										<td>Innkeeper / Hearthmother</td>
										<td>A social hub for resting, gathering rumors, and engaging in downtime events.</td>
										<td>Can trigger character-driven stories, romance hooks, or special conditions.</td>
									</tr>
									<tr>
										<td>Cartographer / Scoutmaster (Optional)</td>
										<td>Helps update the biome map and identify hidden monster zones or paths.</td>
										<td>Players can gain terrain advantages or shortcut routes through favors or aid.</td>
									</tr>
								</tbody>
							</table>
						</div>
					</section>

					<!-- ADAPTING ON THE FLY -->
					<section id="adapting-on-the-fly" class="major-section">
						<h2 class="section-title">🌿 Adapting on the Fly</h2>
						<p>In Untamed, the wild world is constantly shifting.</p>
						<p>As a Game Master, you have the power to breathe life into the environment — making every hunt feel dynamic, reactive, and unpredictable.</p>
						<p>You don't need to plan for every possibility.</p>
						<p>Instead, you can use simple tools like event tables, ecology shifts, and monster behavior rules to respond naturally to player choices and roll with the chaos.</p>

						<h3>🎲 Dynamic Tools You Can Use</h3>
						<p>During hunts, you can trigger changes in the world using these optional tools:</p>
						<div class="table-wrapper">
							<table class="craft-table">
								<thead>
									<tr>
										<th>Tool</th>
										<th>When to Use It</th>
										<th>How It Works</th>
									</tr>
								</thead>
								<tbody>
									<tr>
										<td>Weather Events Table</td>
										<td>Start of hunt, or mid-session when appropriate.</td>
										<td>Roll a d100 on the Biome Random Event Table to generate weather changes like storms, floods, fog, or monster migrations.</td>
									</tr>
									<tr>
										<td>Ecology Shift Table</td>
										<td>After major hunts, monster deaths, or resting periods.</td>
										<td>Roll a d6 on the Ecology Shift Table to introduce big environmental changes like floods, droughts, or monster territory expansions.</td>
									</tr>
									<tr>
										<td>Monster Interaction Events</td>
										<td>When two large creatures meet naturally or share a territory.</td>
										<td>Improvise or use sample interactions (ex: Urmine vs Kragal cliff battles) to create living world moments.</td>
									</tr>
								</tbody>
							</table>
						</div>
					</section>

					<!-- WEATHER EVENTS -->
					<section id="weather-events" class="major-section">
						<h2 class="section-title">🌦 Weather Events</h2>
						<p>Weather is more than flavor — it affects gameplay.</p>

						<h3>Example Weather Impacts:</h3>
						<ul>
							<li><strong>Sudden Rainfall:</strong> Lowers visibility and makes ranged attacks harder.</li>
							<li><strong>Strong Winds:</strong> Makes moving across the Canopy dangerous.</li>
							<li><strong>Dense Fog:</strong> Gives hunters and monsters stealth advantages.</li>
						</ul>
						<p>You can roll randomly or choose events that make the environment feel reactive to what's happening (ex: Storm rolls in after players cause a ruckus).</p>

						<div class="callout"><strong>Pro Tip:</strong> Small weather shifts help keep hunts feeling fresh without bogging down gameplay.</div>
					</section>

					<!-- TERRITORY SHIFTS -->
					<section id="territory-shifts" class="major-section">
						<h2 class="section-title">🌍 Territory Shifts</h2>
						<p>Monsters don't stay in one place forever.</p>
						<p>When an Apex rises, when the river floods, or when a large monster dies — the local ecosystem reacts.</p>

						<h3>Territory Shifts can include:</h3>
						<ul>
							<li>Monsters abandoning areas (leaving new resources behind).</li>
							<li>Predators claiming new zones (altering where encounters happen).</li>
							<li>Safe areas becoming dangerous, or dangerous areas becoming resource-rich.</li>
						</ul>
						<p>Use the Ecology Shift Table when it feels natural to show the world evolving — especially after big fights or long sessions.</p>

						<div class="callout"><strong>Example:</strong> Flooded Hunting Grounds<br />After the hunters slay a river Leviathan, the GM rolls on the Ecology Shift Table and gets "Flooded Lowlands." The once‑fertile hunting grounds become submerged. Herbivores migrate to higher cliffs, attracting new predators. Certain plants rot, while aquatic herbs flourish. Prices for fish drop in nearby villages, but cured meats become scarce. The GM describes muddy currents, half‑drowned burrows, and the distant bellows of displaced beasts. The next hunt starts with the party slogging through knee‑deep water, contending with amphibious monsters that would not normally be there.</div>
					</section>

					<!-- MONSTER INTERACTIONS -->
					<section id="monster-interactions" class="major-section">
						<h2 class="section-title">🐉 Unexpected Monster Interactions</h2>
						<p>Sometimes, monsters don't just react to hunters — they react to each other.</p>

						<h3>You can trigger monster interactions when:</h3>
						<ul>
							<li>Two large monsters are in the same zone.</li>
							<li>A wounded monster retreats into another monster's lair.</li>
							<li>Environmental changes force monsters into new territories.</li>
						</ul>

						<h3>Examples:</h3>
						<ul>
							<li><strong>Territorial Battles:</strong> Urmine battling Kragal for cliff nesting grounds.</li>
							<li><strong>Pack Raiding:</strong> Vorvarg packs swarming an exhausted beast.</li>
							<li><strong>Apex Stirs:</strong> If enough disruption occurs, the hidden Apex might awaken early.</li>
						</ul>
						<p>These moments create dynamic, memorable sessions where players can choose to intervene, exploit the chaos, or retreat.</p>

						<div class="callout"><strong>Example:</strong> Pack Interference<br />The party corners an injured Ironhide Boar near a cliff. Before they can finish it, a Vorvarg pack arrives, drawn by the scent of blood. Following the pack interaction rules, the GM decides the Vorvargs are opportunistic: they target the weakest creature. They circle the wounded boar, nipping at its legs, while one Vorvarg snaps at the hunters to keep them at bay. The boar now has disadvantage on attacks as it fights off two fronts. The hunters can let the predators finish the job (risking a tougher fight later if the pack grows bold) or wade in and risk being flanked. This vignette shows how pack behavior can radically alter a combat's texture without new stat blocks.</div>
					</section>

					<!-- ADAPTATION MINDSET -->
					<section id="adaptation-mindset" class="major-section">
						<h2 class="section-title">🧠 GM Advice: Adaptation Mindset</h2>
						<ul class="check-list">
							<li>Treat the world like a living thing, not a static dungeon.</li>
							<li>Let players discover dynamic shifts naturally (through Perception checks, environmental clues, etc).</li>
							<li>Always keep the hunt focused — but use environmental changes to raise stakes, add tension, or open new opportunities.</li>
							<li>You don't have to "win" — your job is to challenge players and make the world feel real.</li>
						</ul>
					</section>
				</article>
			</div>
		</main>

